{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Reading of the requirement.** `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent` is an ownership statement. TBV owns four things used by its ask builder: the model conversation, the supported backend integrations (`AgentConfig` has Bedrock, OpenAi and Ollama variants), tool dispatch, and structured submission. The finer obligations sit in refining requirements: `isolation`, `provider_failure`, `submission.*` and `fixed_tools`. They need no separate test here.\n\n**Model conversation and structured submission.**\n- `supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport` runs real HTTP against a local server. It asserts the request path, the model, `max_output_tokens`, the four tools in order, and the `submit_result` schema (`required == [\"result\"]`, boolean result). It also checks that the instruction to finish by calling `submit_result` is present, and that the second turn carries the tool output back (`function_call_output`, \"a source snapshot\"). Across two runs it asserts the typed answer, the dependency count, and that no secrets or backend details are persisted.\n- `supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat` makes the same assertions over `/api/chat`. It adds bearer auth, `stream=false`, option pass-through, and delivery of the tool result in a `tool` role message.\n- `behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer` checks that a structured (non-boolean) result via `submit_result` becomes the typed output. It also checks the system and user message content, the tool schema, dependencies, and the evidence round trip.\n\n**Tool dispatch.**\n- `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` checks that graph tools and an application tool are all offered in every request. It checks that results are delivered in order in the same conversation, including paging and multiple calls in one turn. It also checks the recorded observations.\n- `application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call` checks per-call tool state and that the application tool is offered.\n- `source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads` covers dispatch of the source tools.\n\n**Isolation and sharing.**\n- `concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads` and `validator_agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces` run two conversations concurrently. They check that histories, pages and submissions stay separate and that the dependency traces are as expected.\n\n**Backend integrations.**\n- OpenAI and Ollama are exercised end to end over local transports.\n- Bedrock has no end-to-end conversation test. Its backend-specific logic is tested in `providers.rs`:\n  - option typing and the `max_tokens` default;\n  - cache checkpoints;\n  - client tool-call normalization, which preserves order and native history;\n  - SDK retry, credential and profile resolution.\n- A Bedrock conversation needs the AWS service, and `BedrockConfig` has no endpoint field that a test could point at a local server. The shared conversation, dispatch and submission logic that Bedrock reuses is covered through the other backends and the scripted-provider tests.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. The Bedrock tests are marked against this requirement, and the Bedrock-specific code that can be unit tested is tested. The refining requirements carry the detailed obligations, so none are reassessed here.",
  "references": "7c02cb86faa4598ad63d539f0c6a48425e24e8bd62c76651328a325f36426a4e",
